From c9005352d666d94bbc2863908e5611b7634bfa51 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 20:28:11 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] Ad hoc: open the router's ports while hosting (NAT-PMP, then
 UPnP)

Hosting a session now asks the router to forward the session's ports, so
that players on the internet can join without port forwarding by hand: TCP
for matchmaking and the relay, plus any a game adds with
adhoc_host_forward_also() (a port's own UDP link, say). It tries NAT-PMP
(RFC 6886) first, then UPnP (SSDP, the gateway's description, then
AddPortMapping on WANIPConnection or WANPPPConnection). Each port keeps its
number outside. The lease is an hour, renewed at half time, and given back
when hosting stops or the program closes.

The Network page shows the internet address the router reported, with a
button that copies it, or why the router refused. "Open router ports"
(network.router_ports, <PREFIX>_ADHOC_ROUTER_PORTS) turns it off;
<PREFIX>_NO_ROUTER_PORTS=1 does for one run.

Tested: the router-message checks (adhoc_tests --router-messages: NAT-PMP
requests and replies, SSDP, the description, SOAP escaping, a chunked
fault) pass under MSVC. With Dissidia 012's port on Windows 11 behind a
home router with no forwarded ports, NAT-PMP opened TCP 27312, 27313 and
UDP 27420; a Steam Deck on mobile data joined by the address shown, and
the ports were given back on quit. The Network page rows were captured.

Not tested: UPnP mapping in the game (that router answered NAT-PMP
first; its UPnP answered a probe from another machine, while Windows'
firewall kept SSDP replies from an unlisted program on this one),
macOS, and Linux natively.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
